## Summary

The repo at `/home/daniel/sandbox` is the **spring-petclinic-rest** app, built with **Maven** (Maven Wrapper `./mvnw` present; `pom.xml` at root; no Gradle). It has two parallel "arms": the classic Spring repositories and an **OfficeFloor** function-based `POST /api/owners` pipeline. `customerCode` and `membershipNumber` are produced by the OfficeFloor pipeline.

There is a pending change encoded in the acceptance test: the `customerCode` format must change from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`.

---

## 1. Where customerCode is generated/formatted

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`** (lines 16-25) — the only place it is computed:

```java
public class AssignCustomerCode {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        String lastName = owner.getLastName();
        int length = Math.min(3, lastName.length());
        String prefix = lastName.substring(0, length).toUpperCase();
        int sequence = ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1;
        owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%04d", prefix, sequence));
    }
}
```

Current format: `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (e.g. `SMI-0007`), where `NNNN` = `findAll().size() + 1`, zero-padded to 4 digits. Note: `city` is **not** currently used here — the pending change (per the test) needs a `<CITY3>` prefix added, read from `owner.getCity()`.

The Javadoc (lines 7-14) documents the current behavior and notes it runs after `BuildOwner`, before `SaveOwner`, so the count excludes the owner being created.

---

## 2. Everything derived from customerCode

**membershipNumber** — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignMembershipNumber.java` (lines 15-21):

```java
public class AssignMembershipNumber {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
        String yy = String.format("%02d", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100);
        owner.setMembershipNumber(owner.getCustomerCode() + "-M" + yy);
    }
}
```

Format: `<customerCode>-M<YY>` (e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`), where `YY` is last two digits of `registrationDate` year. Because it concatenates `customerCode` verbatim, changing the customerCode format automatically flows into the membership number.

`registrationDate` is defaulted in **`BuildOwner.java`** (lines 15-17) to `LocalDate.now()` when null, so `AssignMembershipNumber` always has a year.

**membershipTier** — derived at *read time* (not stored), in the mapper `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` (lines 26, 33-37):

```java
@Mapping(target = "membershipTier", expression = "java(membershipTier(owner))")
OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);

default String membershipTier(Owner owner) {
    boolean silver = owner.getNamesakeCount() != null && owner.getNamesakeCount() == 0
        && owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank();
    return silver ? "SILVER" : "BRONZE";
}
```

`membershipTier` derives from `namesakeCount` + `email`, not from `customerCode`, but it is membership-related.

**Pipeline order** — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` (lines 24-47): `build` → `assignNamesakeCount` → `assignCustomerCode` → `assignMembershipNumber` → `assignHousehold` → `save`. All run under `govern: [ transaction ]`. So `customerCode` is assigned before `membershipNumber`, and both are set on the `Owner` before `SaveOwner`.

**OpenAPI docs** — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`: `customerCode` (lines 2094-2102), `membershipNumber` (lines 2122-2130), `membershipTier` (lines 2131-2138). All marked `readOnly: true`. These descriptions still state the OLD `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` format and would need updating alongside a format change.

---

## 3. Tests referencing customerCode / membership

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** (full file, 16 lines):

```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.acceptance;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;
import tools.jackson.databind.JsonNode;

/** customer-code-city: Change the customerCode format to '<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where CITY3 is the upper-cased... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix() throws Exception {
		int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
		JsonNode n = fetchOwner(id);
		assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}")); // <CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>
	}
}
```

This asserts the NEW format `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\d{4}`, which the current `AssignCustomerCode` does **not** produce (it only emits `[A-Z]{1,3}-\d{4}`). This test currently fails and is the target of the pending `customer-code-city` change.

The base class **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`** (322 lines) provides the harness. Relevant parts:
- `ownerNode()` (lines 117-125): builds a valid owner with `firstName="Test"`, unique `lastName` (`"Sur"+letters`), unique `address`, unique `city` (`"Town"+seq`, lines 94-96), unique `telephone`.
- `createOwnerOk(...)` (lines 160-162), `fetchOwner(int)` (lines 200-204), `extractId(...)` (lines 206-219).
- `fillCity(String city, int n)` (lines 173-179): creates `n` owners all in the same city — used by per-city rules.
- Class-level `@Transactional` rollback (line 49); `@WithMockUser` admin (line 50).
- The class Javadoc (lines 38-40) explicitly lists "membership number, per-day cap, per-city cap, 'most common city'" as count/quota rules.
- Note: with `uniqueCity()`, every test owner is in a brand-new city, so a `<CITY3>` prefix derives from `"Town..."` → `"TOW"`, matching `[A-Z]{3}`.

No unit tests exist for the individual `Assign*` functions; `AcceptanceTest` is the only test referencing these fields.

---

## 4. The Owner entity and city/lastName fields

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`**:
- `city` field: lines 42-44 (`@Column(name="city")`, `@NotEmpty`), getter `getCity()` lines 81-83, setter lines 85-87.
- `customerCode`: field lines 58-59, getter/setter lines 113-119.
- `membershipNumber`: field lines 61-62, getter/setter lines 121-127.
- `householdId`: lines 64-65, 129-135. `namesakeCount`: lines 67-68, 137-143.
- `registrationDate`: lines 55-56, 105-111.
- `lastName`/`firstName` are **inherited from `Person`** (not declared here; referenced via `getLastName()`/`getFirstName()`, e.g. toString lines 211-212). Base class: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java`.

DB schema (owners table) in all four dialects, e.g. `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/h2/schema.sql` (lines 31-44): `city VARCHAR(80) NOT NULL` (line 36), `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` (line 40), `membership_number VARCHAR(32)` (line 41). Same columns in `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` (47-48), `db/mysql/schema.sql` (37-38), and `db/postgres/schema.sql` (35-36, typed `TEXT`). Note: h2/hsqldb/mysql cap `customer_code` at `VARCHAR(20)` — the longer `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` format (max ~12 chars) still fits.

City is also set from the request in **`ApplyOwner.java`** line 11 (`owner.setCity(request.getCity())`) and validated non-blank in **`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** line 50.

---

## 5. How owners are queried by city

There is **no** repository method that counts or queries owners by city. The `OwnerRepository` interface (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`) exposes only:
- `findByLastName(String)` and paged variant (lines 46, 48)
- `findById(int)` (line 57)
- `save(Owner)` (line 66)
- `findAll()` and paged variant (lines 74, 76)
- `delete(Owner)` (line 84)

No `countByCity`, `findByCity`, or similar exists anywhere (grep across `src/main` repository package and the function package found nothing).

The established pattern for count-based rules is to iterate `ownerRepository.findAll()` in memory. See **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (lines 16-27), which counts existing owners matching first+last name:

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    String firstName = owner.getFirstName();
    String lastName = owner.getLastName();
    int count = 0;
    for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
        if (firstName.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getFirstName())
                && lastName.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getLastName())) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    owner.setNamesakeCount(count);
}
```

`AssignHousehold.java` (lines 32-39) and `AssignCustomerCode.java` (line 22) likewise iterate / size `findAll()`. So a per-city count would follow this same `findAll()` + `getCity()` filtering approach rather than a dedicated repo query. Repository implementations exist at `.../repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `.../repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, and `.../repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (none has a city method).

---

## Build tool & running tests

- **Build tool: Maven**, via the wrapper `/home/daniel/sandbox/mvnw` (and `mvnw.cmd`), config in `/home/daniel/sandbox/.mvn`. `pom.xml` at repo root. No Gradle files.
- Per `readme.md`: build with `./mvnw clean install` (line 218 shows `mvn clean install`); run app with `./mvnw spring-boot:run` (line 25). Some classes (DTOs from `openapi.yml`, MapStruct mappers) are **generated at build time** (readme lines 195-218), so tests must be run through Maven, not a bare `javac`.
- Run all tests: `./mvnw test`. Run just the acceptance test: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`. A full `./mvnw verify` / `install` also runs them.
- Tests use JUnit 5 (`org.junit.jupiter`) with Spring Boot `@SpringBootTest` + `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, and Jackson's new `tools.jackson` package.

One correctness note for the pending change: `AssignCustomerCode` uses `lastName.substring(0, min(3,len))`, so it tolerates short last names, but a `<CITY3>` prefix built the same way from `owner.getCity()` should apply the same `Math.min` guard (and filter out non-letters / uppercase) to keep matching the test's `[A-Z]{3}` expectation for arbitrary cities.

